  "_note": "THE LEGACY VERB CORPUS (legacy-package.md) \u2014 one fixture per DECLARED v3 verb: the v3 call shapes in, the expected modern routing ({chain, action, args template}) and the expected v3 response convention out. The v3 API is a CLOSED FINITE set (the owner's enumerate-never-parse law), so this corpus is EXHAUSTIVE over the declared table, not representative. Runners: (1) ruby \u2014 dsx_graph_test.rb asserts the tree's aggregated facets.legacy declarations equal these fixtures (drift in either direction fails); (2) node \u2014 the shim harness drives the GENERATED page shim per verb and asserts the modern wire envelope + the response delivery; (3) native \u2014 LegacyConformance (OpenSource/Engine/iOS/ConformanceHosts.swift, driven by the record lane's RecordMain.swift) replays every `calls` string through the closed-table ROUTER fold the native routers implement \u2014 prefix split, args template, response pick \u2014 and asserts it lands on this row. The Kotlin :app twin is the one remaining ledgered leg (legacy-package.md \u00a7follow-ups). `owner` names the declaring module folder; `defaultExcluded` marks verbs whose owner ships excluded on the template excluded.json (the shim keeps their function and answers the honest not_loaded envelope).",
  "verbs": {
    "writevalue": {
      "owner": "Core/Basics/ValueStore",
      "calls": [
        "window.despia = \"writevalue://<tail>\"",
        "despia(\"writevalue://<tail>\")",
        "window.despia.writevalue(<tail>)",
        "location.href = \"writevalue://<tail>\" (navigation router)",
        "<appscheme-free deeplink> writevalue://<tail> (openURL router)"
      ],
      "route": {
        "chain": "writevalue",
        "action": "write",
        "args": {
          "value": "$tail"
        }
      },
      "response": null
    },
    "readvalue": {
      "owner": "Core/Basics/ValueStore",
